Give bash's unwind-protect cleanups the type they are actually called with.

`Function' is bash's K&R `int ()', so `(*cleanup) (arg)' in unwind_prot.c
compiles to an indirect call returning int with one argument, whatever was
registered. On a normal ABI that mismatch is invisible: a surplus argument sits
unread in a register, and a void function just leaves the return register
undefined. wasm types its indirect calls and checks them at the call site, so
this traps with "indirect call type mismatch" — and since every cleanup bash
registers returns void, nothing ever unwound. In practice `bash -c` died at the
end of parse_and_execute, on pop_stream.

Calling through the shape the cleanups genuinely have, `void (char *)', fixes
all but two kinds, which cannot be spelled that way and get a wrapper at each
registration site: those taking no argument at all, and the two that return int
(close and restore_signal_mask). `void (int)' needs no wrapper — an int and a
pointer are both i32 on wasm32, so it already is that type.

--- a/unwind_prot.c
+++ b/unwind_prot.c
@@ -87,6 +87,23 @@
 static inline void restore_variable PARAMS((SAVED_VAR *));
 static void unwind_protect_mem_internal PARAMS((char *, char *));
 
+/* The shape every cleanup is actually called with.
+ *
+ * `Function' is bash's K&R `int ()', so `(*cleanup) (arg)' compiles to a call
+ * returning int with one argument no matter what was registered. On a normal
+ * ABI the mismatch is invisible: a surplus argument sits unread in a register
+ * and a void function just leaves the return register undefined. wasm types
+ * its indirect calls and checks them at the call, so calling a `void (char *)'
+ * through an `int ()' pointer traps with "indirect call type mismatch" — and
+ * every cleanup bash registers returns void, so nothing would ever unwind.
+ *
+ * Casting here to the shape the cleanups genuinely have fixes all but two
+ * kinds, which cannot be expressed this way and are wrapped at their
+ * registration sites instead (grep for uw_): those taking no argument, and
+ * the two that return int. Note `void (int)' needs no wrapper — an int and a
+ * pointer are both i32 on wasm32, so it is already this exact type. */
+typedef void sh_uwcleanup_t PARAMS((char *));
+
 static UNWIND_ELT *unwind_protect_list = (UNWIND_ELT *)NULL;
 
 /* Allocating from a cache of unwind-protect elements */
@@ -323,7 +340,7 @@
